{
  "_comment": "The real car models the game draws (tools/prepare_car_models.py -> game/assets/models/<car>.glb), per catalogue car. file: the source in unreal/Import/Models (CC BY, its LICENSE.txt beside it); forward: the model's own nose axis; tyres: its tyre nodes (name or prefix); fixed: node-name parts that stay with the body though they sit at a wheel; drop: node-name parts left out; interior: false when the model has no cabin (the game builds its own inside the shell); steering: node-name parts of the steering wheel; mirror: of the rear-view mirror's housing (the game's glass goes on it), mirror_glass: of the model's own mirror glass (the game's glass covers it, at its size), mirror_box: that glass found by hand ({centre, size}, car frame), none of them: the game hangs its own mirror where the driver sees it; eye, dials (offset, width, past), mirror_offset: corrections in metres, car frame (x right, y up, z back). eye_at: the eyes found by hand, car frame. frame: the geometry eye_at and mirror_box were read off the prepared model in ({front, rear, hub_y}: the axles' distances from the reference point and the rest hub height, as SPECS had them then); the preparation carries them to the present geometry, as the model is scaled to the wheelbase about its front hubs and hub plane (dims6: the E-Class's box, read at the Egea's wheelbase, hung the glass 7.5 cm under its housing at the W212's). same_as: draw another car's prepared model (the game fits the wheels to this car's tyres). cabin_from: {car: a donor entry (a key starting with _ is only a donor), nodes, offset, scale, inside_only}: a shell without a cabin takes the donor's parts; inside_only: those of them drawn only from the driver's seat (they poke through the shell). brake: material-name parts of the rear lamps that light when braking; keep: materials left untouched though their names say glass. paint_atlas: {material: {key, tolerance}}: materials whose texture holds the paint with the rest of the car (lamps, trim, badges), recoloured by the game where the texture is the key colour (sRGB 0-255, measured from the texture; refined by the preparation) within the tolerance of chromaticity (default 0.06).",
